;; ============================================================================

;; CONTROL FLOW  —  port of Simple's RegionNode, IfNode, LoopNode, CProjNode,
;;                  StartNode, StopNode, ReturnNode, XCtrlNode, NeverNode
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; These are one file because their peepholes are one system: a Region with a dead path drops it,
;; an If on a constant kills one projection, that projection turns to XCTRL, and the Region that
;; merged it drops a path in turn. Dead control propagates through all of them, and splitting the
;; halves of that cascade across ten files puts one rewrite in ten places.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; REGION — the control merge, and the most peephole-dense node in the graph
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; Inputs 1..n are the incoming control paths. Input 0 is unused (it is where a data node keeps its
;; control, and a Region has none).
;;
;; ## compute
;;
;;     if in-progress: CONTROL          (see the construction window below)
;;     else:           XCONTROL meet over all inputs
;;
;; Seeding with XCONTROL and meeting up is the whole of reachability: a Region is reachable exactly
;; when SOME path reaching it is. Note the direction — this is the optimistic seed, and a Region
;; nobody has analysed yet reports "unreachable" until evidence arrives.
;;
;; > THE TRAP, AND IT IS THE PROVEN-TYPE LAW IN CONCRETE FORM: seeding with XCONTROL means a merge
;; > that nobody has analysed reports PROVABLY UNREACHABLE out of nothing. `~ctrl` is merely the
;; > high element of the control axis, not a claim anyone computed. A rewrite that acts on it is
;; > acting on the absence of information. Any irreversible rewrite must ask `ty-proven?` first.
;;
;; ## idealize — Simple's rules, in the order it applies them
;;
;; 1. IN-PROGRESS: do nothing at all. Bail before every other rule.
;;
;; 2. DEAD PATH. Find an input whose type is XCONTROL and remove it — and with it the matching
;;    value from EVERY Phi on this Region, in the same operation.
;;
;;    Simple's `removeDeadPath` cannot use the obvious output iterator: deleting a Phi input can
;;    recursively delete the Phi ITSELF, which shuffles the output array and makes the loop skip an
;;    unrelated Phi. So it repeats the scan until the output count stops changing. Port that loop
;;    faithfully; the naive version drops a Phi arm on rare occasions, which is a miscompile that
;;    typechecks.
;;
;;    Exception: never delete the ENTRY path of a Loop. Removing the back edge and leaving a
;;    single-entry Region is fine and folds the Loop away; removing the entry instead promotes the
;;    back edge to entry and corrupts the loop structure.
;;
;; 3. SINGLE INPUT: `nIns()==2 && !hasPhi()` -> become that input. Guarded on having no Phis
;;    because a one-input Phi collapses on its own, and doing both at once races.
;;
;;    >>> THIS RULE IS WHAT MAKES INLINING WORK. A Fun IS a Region and a Parm IS a Phi, so
;;    >>> "inlining" is: bypass the Call, leave the Fun with one input, and let THIS rule collapse
;;    >>> it while the Parms collapse into the caller's actual arguments. Nothing is cloned. See
;;    >>> src/node/call.coil. If this rule is weakened, inlining silently stops happening.
;;
;; 4. EMPTY DIAMOND: `if (pred) {} else {}` — a Region with no Phi users whose two inputs are both
;;    CProjs of the SAME If. Replace with that If's control directly. This is what deletes a
;;    conditional whose arms did nothing.
;;
;; 5. FLATTEN STACKED REGIONS: a Region feeding a Region, neither a Loop, with no value stranded
;;    between them. Splice the inner one's paths into the outer and extend every Phi accordingly.
;;    `hasMidUser` is the guard: if anything between the two Regions uses a value from the inner one
;;    other than a Phi on the outer, Simple declines — "difficult to correctly fuse without
;;    extensive testing, so just ignore".
;;
;; ## The in-progress window  (LAW, docs/LAYOUT.md §8)
;;
;; A Region is IN PROGRESS while its last input is null. While in progress it reports CONTROL
;; unconditionally, its Phis report their DECLARED types, and it is never GVN-equal to anything
;; (`eq` returns `!inProgress()`).
;;
;; A loop body must be built AND PEEPHOLED inside that window. Final Simple's `endLoop` then closes
;; control and immediately fills every Phi backedge as one protected operation. A Phi's independent
;; null-final-input window prevents folding during that interval; no peephole belongs between the
;; two mutations.
;;
;; ## Region and Phi arity are ONE invariant
;;
;; Dropping a path drops the matching value from every Phi on that Region, in the same operation.
;; There is deliberately no `region-del-path!` that does not also touch the Phis. Diverging for even
;; one peephole gives a Phi reading the wrong arm — a miscompile that typechecks cleanly.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; IF — the two-way branch
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; Inputs: 0 = control, 1 = predicate. It is a MULTI node: it produces a tuple, and the two CProjs
;; select from it. `uctrl` is deliberately null — there is no single successor.
;;
;; ## compute  — returns one of four TypeTuples
;;
;;     ctrl not reachable        -> IF_NEITHER
;;     predicate type is HIGH    -> IF_NEITHER   (wait for it to fall; do not guess a side)
;;     predicate is constant     -> IF_FALSE if zero/null/empty-fidx, else IF_TRUE
;;     adding zero changes it    -> IF_TRUE      (the predicate provably cannot be zero/null)
;;     otherwise                 -> IF_BOTH
;;
;; The fourth case is the one worth noticing: `!t.makeZero().isa(t)` asks "would admitting null
;; widen this type?" If yes, null was not in it, so the test cannot fail. That is how a null check
;; on an already-non-null reference deletes itself, and it is the mechanism a discharged TypeScript
;; annotation rides on.
;;
;; ## idealize — dominating identical test
;;
;; Walk UP the immediate-dominator tree. If an enclosing If tests the same predicate, then on this
;; branch the answer is already known: replace our predicate with the constant 1 or 0 depending on
;; which CProj we came down through. This is how a repeated bounds check or repeated null check
;; folds away, and it costs one dominator walk rather than a dataflow pass.
;;
;; ## The multi window  (LAW, docs/LAYOUT.md §8)
;;
;; An If is IN PROGRESS until ALL of its projections exist. Peephole them one at a time and the
;; first folds to ~ctrl, the If loses its last use, the kill cascade takes it, and the sibling then
;; rewrites against a corpse whose inputs were cleared but whose type was not — a stale buffer word.
;; Flat, that word happened to be pinned Start and the answer was accidentally right; one level of
;; nesting down it was a dead node.
;;
;; `if-arms!` is therefore the ONLY sanctioned constructor: it opens the window, builds both CProjs,
;; and closes it. There is deliberately no way to make one arm alone.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; LOOP, START, STOP, RETURN, CPROJ, XCTRL, NEVER
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; LOOP is a Region whose input 1 is the entry and input 2 is the BACK EDGE. Everything Region does
;; applies; the differences are that the entry path is never deleted, and that a Phi on a Loop gets
;; the widening treatment described in src/node/phi.coil.
;;
;; START is the root and a MULTI: it projects the initial control, the initial memory, and the
;; arguments. Constants are pinned to it, which is why every use of the same constant GVNs to one
;; node in the start block.
;;
;; STOP is the sink. Every Return is an input. The program is exactly what Stop keeps alive — which
;; is why a dropped Return is invisible to the verifier: a Stop with fewer inputs is a structurally
;; valid Stop.
;;
;; RETURN carries control, memory, the value, and RPC. Inlining nulls the RPC edge; the enclosing
;; Fun remains separate node metadata.
;;
;; CPROJ selects one control edge out of a multi. It is a block head.
;;
;; XCTRL is the unreachable-control constant — the value a folded-away branch becomes.
;;
;; NEVER is control that cannot be PROVEN dead but is known not to return, e.g. an infinite loop
;; that the loop-tree pass broke. GCM's `better` treats it as maximally good placement, so nothing
;; is ever hoisted past it.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; STATUS: implemented. Every rule above is live and tested. What is NOT here, and why:
;;   - Fun/Parm (a Region and a Phi) and Call/CallEnd  -> the calls unit
;;   - Loop `forceExit` for infinite loops             -> needs the loop tree
;;   - Start's memory and argument projections          -> need memory types
;; Each is named where it would go, and reaching one is a hard error, never a quiet default.
;; ============================================================================
(module aot.node.control)
(import "aot.util.arena" :use [compiler-allocator])
(import "coil.primitive" :as primitive)
(import "coil.alloc" :as alloc :use [unwrap-ptr create box!])
(import "coil.arraylist" :use *)
(import "coil.hashmap" :use *)
(import "aot.util.panic" :use *)
(import "aot.node.node" :use *)
(import "aot.node.cfg" :use *)
(import "aot.node.constant" :use *)
(import "aot.type.type" :use *)
(import "aot.node.region" :use * :reexport)
(import "aot.node.cfgof" :use * :reexport)

;; If projection indices. Simple: index 0 is the TRUE arm, index 1 is the FALSE arm.
(const IF-TRUE-IDX 0)

(const IF-FALSE-IDX 1)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; START
;;
;; The external world calling in. Simple's Start is a LoopNode (its input 1 is the Stop, closing
;; what Simple calls the Grand Cycle between the code we know and the code we do not), and a Multi:
;; it projects the initial control, the initial memory and the arguments.
;;
;; Here it is a Multi with no dominator and idepth 0. Its fixed members are control, bulk memory,
;; and the external argument. Keeping memory at index 1 matches Call and Return state tuples.
(defstruct StartNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (arg-ty i64)])

;; Start's ordinary memory projection describes an arbitrary incoming realm. Only the process
;; entry wrapper consumes this additional projection, after runtime boot of a fresh heap image.
;; Keeping them separate prevents external callable roots from inheriting initialization facts.
(const START-INITIAL-MEM 3)

(impl NodeOps StartNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr StartNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Start")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; Start is the root of the program. Folding it to a constant would delete the world.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr StartNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr StartNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (ty-tuple-of4 TY-CTRL
                         (ty-mem 1 TY-BOT)
                         (.arg-ty self)
                         (ty-mem 1 TY-BOT)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr StartNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  ;; Final Simple owns one Start per compilation unit, so two Start identities never meet in GVN.
  ;; Source and JSL graphs currently have distinct Starts; preserve that root identity explicitly
  ;; or equal-shaped Starts with different argument contracts can subsume one another during Iter.
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr StartNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (n-same? (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) other))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr StartNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (+ 101 (n-nid (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))))

(impl CFGOps StartNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr StartNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  (block-head? [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> bool) true)
  ;; No immediate dominator, and idepth 0. Every dominator walk terminates here.
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr StartNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> i64) 0))

(defn start-make [(arg-ty i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (compiler-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [StartNode] (alloc/create [StartNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.arg-ty p) arg-ty)
    (n-init! d OP-START)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; STOP
;;
;; The sink. Every Return is an input, and the program is exactly what Stop keeps alive — which is
;; why a dropped Return is invisible to the verifier: a Stop with fewer inputs is a structurally
;; valid Stop. Only running the program notices.
;;
;; `idealize` drops inputs that have gone dead (Simple tests `in(i).nIns()==0`), and reports progress
;; only if the count actually changed.
(defstruct StopNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (pad i64)])

(impl NodeOps StopNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr StopNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Stop")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr StopNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> i64) TY-CTRL)
  (idealize [(self (ptr StopNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (stop-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr StopNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (= (n-op other) OP-STOP))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> i64) 103))

(impl CFGOps StopNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr StopNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; Final Simple treats Stop as the synthetic head of the sink block. It can have several Return
  ;; predecessors, so no single immediate dominator exists.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr StopNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr StopNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (let [d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)
                    cnt (n-nins d)
                    (mut i) 0
                    (mut depth) 0]
                (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                          (break 0)
                          (do
                            (match (n-in d (load i))
                              (Some [ret]
                                    (match (cfg-of ret)
                                      (Some [_]
                                            (let [candidate
                                                    (+ (cfg-idepth-of ret) 1)]
                                              (if (> candidate (load depth))
                                                  (set! (mut depth) candidate)
                                                  0)))
                                      (None [] 0)))
                              (None [] 0))
                            (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                            (continue))))
                (load depth))))

;; Drop every input that has gone dead. Reports progress only when the count changed.
(defn stop-idealize [(d (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [before (n-nins d)
        (mut i) 0]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins d))
              (break 0)
              (match (n-in d (load i))
                (Some [v]
                      (if (n-dead? v)
                          (do (n-del-def! d (load i)) (continue))
                          (do (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1)) (continue))))
                (None [] (do (n-del-def! d (load i)) (continue))))))
    (if (!= before (n-nins d)) (Some d) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))

(defn stop-make [] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (compiler-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [StopNode] (alloc/create [StopNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.pad p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-STOP)
    (n-set-ty! d TY-CTRL)
    d))

(defn stop-add-return! [(stop (dyn NodeOps)) (ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (n-add-def! stop (Some ret)) 0))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; XCTRL — the unreachable-control constant
;;
;; What a folded-away branch becomes. `is-const?` is true so the engine never tries to replace it
;; with a Constant, which would loop.
(defstruct XCtrlNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (pad i64)])

(impl NodeOps XCtrlNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "XCtrl")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr XCtrlNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> i64) TY-XCTRL)
  (idealize [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr XCtrlNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (= (n-op other) OP-XCTRL))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> i64) 107))

(impl CFGOps XCtrlNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  (block-head? [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> i64) 0))

(defn xctrl-make [] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (compiler-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [XCtrlNode] (alloc/create [XCtrlNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.pad p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-XCTRL)
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (n-set-ty! d TY-XCTRL)
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; IF — the two-way branch
;;
;; Inputs: 0 = control, 1 = predicate. A MULTI: it produces a two-member tuple, and the two CProjs
;; select from it. Member 0 is TRUE, member 1 is FALSE.
(defstruct IfNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (never bool)])

(defn if-ctrl [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! n 0))

(defn if-pred [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (n-in n 1))

;; Simple's four outcomes.
;;
;;   ctrl not reachable      -> IF_NEITHER
;;   predicate HIGH          -> IF_NEITHER   (wait for it to fall; do NOT guess a side)
;;   predicate constant      -> IF_FALSE for JavaScript's falsy constants, else IF_TRUE
;;   adding zero widens it   -> IF_TRUE      (the predicate provably cannot be zero)
;;   otherwise               -> IF_BOTH
;;
;; The fourth case is the interesting one: "would admitting zero widen this type?" If yes, zero was
;; not in it, so the test cannot fail. That is how a null check on an already-non-null reference
;; deletes itself, and it is the mechanism a discharged TypeScript annotation rides on.
;;
;; IF_NEITHER from a high predicate is NOT "both arms are dead". It means nobody has decided yet.
(defn if-compute [(d (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [ct (n-ty (if-ctrl d))]
    (if (and (!= ct TY-CTRL) (!= ct TY-BOT))
        (ty-if-neither)
        ;; A Never has no predicate: it is control that cannot be proven dead.
        (match (if-pred d)
          (None [] (ty-if-both))
          (Some [p]
                (let [t
                      ;; JavaScript source constants cross semantic boundaries boxed. Truthiness
                      ;; observes the payload value, not merely the singleton representation tag;
                      ;; otherwise Box(0) and Box(1) are both just `dyn:int` and neither branch can
                      ;; fold. This is the JS-specific extension of Simple's constant predicate
                      ;; rule, expressed structurally so Box remains the dynamic value.
                      (if (and (= (n-op p) OP-BOX) (> (n-nins p) 1))
                          (n-ty (n-in! p 1))
                          (n-ty p))]
                  (if (ty-high? t)
                      (ty-if-neither)
                      (if (ty-constant? t)
                          (if (ty-js-falsy-constant? t)
                              (ty-if-false)
                              (ty-if-true))
                          ;; Does admitting zero widen the type? If so, zero was not in it.
                          (if (and (ty-int? t) (not (ty-isa? (ty-int-con 0) t)))
                              (ty-if-true)
                              (ty-if-both))))))))))

;; Hunt UP the dominator tree for an enclosing If on the SAME predicate. On this branch the answer
;; is already known, so replace the predicate with the constant that arm implies.
;;
;; This is how a repeated bounds check or null check folds away — one dominator walk, not a dataflow
;; pass. `n-add-dep!` on each dominator visited, so a later change up the chain retries this.
(defn if--true-arm-cast-source [(candidate (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (!= (n-op candidate) OP-CAST)
      (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
      (match (n-in candidate 0)
        (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
        (Some [ctrl]
              (if (or (!= (n-op ctrl) OP-CPROJ)
                      (!= (cproj-idx ctrl) IF-TRUE-IDX))
                  (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
                  (match (n-in candidate 1)
                    (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
                    (Some [source]
                          (let [iff (n-in! ctrl 0)]
                            (if (and (= (n-op iff) OP-IF)
                                     (match (if-pred iff)
                                       (Some [tested] (n-same? tested source))
                                       (None [] false)))
                                (Some source)
                                (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))))))))

;; Final Simple IfNode.idealize walks from idom to the root, registering dependencies,
;; and folds a matching predicate (or its nonzero Guard) on a dominating CProj arm.
;; Simple's GCM schedEarly places a value no earlier than its inputs; Phis are pinned
;; and their backedges are not traversed. Apply that availability rule to this search:
;; any one transitive data input supplies a safe boundary, not necessarily the deepest.
;; Follow one nonconstant input chain, stopping at pinned control (including Phi's
;; Region and a call result's CallEnd). Never follow CFG predecessors or Phi backedges.
;; Cast's control is deliberately ignored: the matching rule also tests its unguarded
;; source, whose definition, not the guard, must bound the search. Watch every traversed
;; value so rewrites invalidate the answer. No cached dominance facts or search budget.
(defn if--predicate-definition [(pred (dyn NodeOps)) (watcher (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [(mut current) (Some pred)
        (mut answer) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
        (mut steps) 0]
    (loop (match (load current)
            (None [] (break 0))
            (Some [value]
                  (do
                    (if (> (load steps) (arena-node-count))
                        (broken "if--predicate-definition: cyclic unpinned data chain")
                        0)
                    (set! (mut steps) (+ (load steps) 1))
                    (n-add-dep! value watcher)
                    (set! (mut current) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
                    (if (and (!= (n-op value) OP-CAST) (is-pinned? value))
                        (match (n-in-safe value 0)
                          (Some [producer]
                                (match (cfg-of producer)
                                  (Some [_] (set! (mut answer) (Some producer)))
                                  (None [] (set! (mut current) (Some producer)))))
                          (None [] 0))
                        (let [(mut i) 1]
                          (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins value))
                                    (break 0)
                                    (do
                                      (match (n-in value (load i))
                                        (Some [input]
                                              (if (not (is-const? input))
                                                  (do
                                                    (set! (mut current)
                                                          (Some input))
                                                    (break 0))
                                                  0))
                                        (None [] 0))
                                      (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                                      (continue))))))
                    (match (load answer)
                      (Some [_] (break 0))
                      (None [] (continue)))))))
    (load answer)))

(defn if-idealize [(d (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (match (if-pred d)
    (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (Some [pred]
          (if (ty-high-or-const? (n-ty pred))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
              (let [definition (if--predicate-definition pred d)
                    (mut prior) (Some d)
                    (mut dom) (cfg-idom-of d)
                    (mut out) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
                    (mut guard) 0]
                (loop (if (> (load guard) 100000)
                          (do
                            (broken "if-idealize: dominator walk did not converge")
                            (break 0))
                          (match (load dom)
                            (None [] (break 0))
                            (Some [dm]
                                  (do
                                    (n-add-dep! dm d)
                                    (if (match definition
                                          (Some [anchor] (n-same? anchor dm))
                                          (None [] false))
                                        (break 0)
                                        0)
                                    (if (and (= (n-op dm) OP-IF)
                                             (match (load prior)
                                               (Some [pr]
                                                     (= (n-op pr) OP-CPROJ))
                                               (None [] false)))
                                        (match (if-pred dm)
                                          (None [] 0)
                                          (Some [dp]
                                                (if (or (n-same? dp pred)
                                                        (match (if--true-arm-cast-source pred)
                                                          (Some [source]
                                                                (n-same? dp
                                                                         source))
                                                          (None [] false)))
                                                    (do
                                                      (n-set-def! d
                                                                  1
                                                                  (Some (con-int (if (= (match (load prior)
                                                                                          (Some [pr]
                                                                                                (cproj-idx pr))
                                                                                          (None []
                                                                                                0))
                                                                                        IF-TRUE-IDX)
                                                                                     1
                                                                                     0))))
                                                      (set! (mut out) (Some d))
                                                      (break 0))
                                                    0)))
                                        0)
                                    (set! (mut prior) (Some dm))
                                    (set! (mut dom) (cfg-idom-of dm))
                                    (set! (mut guard) (+ (load guard) 1))
                                    (continue))))))
                (load out))))))

(impl NodeOps IfNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr IfNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (if (.never self) "Never" "If"))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; An If's type is a TUPLE, never constant, so the engine would not fold it anyway — but say so
  ;; explicitly rather than relying on that.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr IfNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  ;; A Never is "never true", used to give an infinite loop an exit so that Stop stays reachable.
  ;; It always reports IF_BOTH and never idealizes.
  (compute [(self (ptr IfNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (if (.never self)
               (ty-if-both)
               (if-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))
  (idealize [(self (ptr IfNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (if (.never self)
                (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
                (if-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr IfNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (or (= (n-op other) OP-IF) (= (n-op other) OP-NEVER))
                (= (.never self)
                   (.never (primitive/cast (ptr IfNode) (hdr other))))
                false))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> i64) (if (.never self) 131 127)))

(impl CFGOps IfNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr IfNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; An If is a block TAIL. Its CProjs are the heads.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr IfNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr IfNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

(defn if--alloc [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps))
                 (pred (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
                 (never bool)
                 (opcode i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (compiler-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [IfNode] (alloc/create [IfNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.never p) never)
    (n-init! d opcode)
    (n-add-def! d (Some ctrl))
    (n-add-def! d pred)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

;; Build an If. NOT peepholed: the caller must build BOTH arms first — see `if-arms!`.
(defn if-make [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps)) (pred (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (if--alloc ctrl (Some pred) false OP-IF))

;; "Never true", for giving an infinite loop an exit so that Stop stays reachable.
(defn never-make [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (if--alloc ctrl (None [(dyn NodeOps)]) true OP-NEVER))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; PROJECTIONS — selecting one member out of a MULTI
;;
;; CProj selects a CONTROL member and is itself a control node and a block HEAD. Proj selects a
;; VALUE member and is a data node, PINNED to its multi's block.
;;
;; Both carry the member index and a debugging label, and both fold into the index — Simple hashes
;; and compares on `_idx` alone.
(defstruct CProjNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (idx i64) (name (slice u8))])

(defstruct ProjNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (idx i64) (name (slice u8))])

(defn cproj-idx [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (!= (n-op n) OP-CPROJ)
      (do (panic "cproj-idx: not a CProj") 0)
      (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr CProjNode) (hdr n)))))

(defn proj-idx [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (!= (n-op n) OP-PROJ)
      (do (panic "proj-idx: not a Proj") 0)
      (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ProjNode) (hdr n)))))

;; Read member `idx` out of the multi's tuple. When the multi is not (yet) a tuple, fall back to its
;; own extreme — `ty-oob`. High stays high because nothing is known yet; low goes to the bottom
;; because anything could be there.
(defn proj--compute [(d (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> i64)
  (match (n-in d 0)
    (None [] TY-TOP)
    (Some [m]
          (let [t (n-ty m)]
            (if (ty-tuple? t)
                (if (>= idx (ty-tuple-len t)) (ty-oob t) (ty-tuple-at t idx))
                (ty-oob t))))))

;; Simple's MultiNode.pcopy hook.  Most multis have no alternate source for a projection; call
;; multis live in a later module and contribute their implementation through the registered
;; extension at the bottom of this file, just as they do for `cfg-of`.
(defn multi-pcopy [(m (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (or (= (n-op m) OP-CALLEND)
          (= (n-op m) OP-PROPLOAD)
          (= (n-op m) OP-PROPSTORE))
      (pcopy-ext m idx)
      (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))

;; A CProj whose member is XCONTROL is DEAD and becomes the XCtrl node.
;;
;; And Simple's second rule: for an If whose OTHER arm is XCONTROL, this arm is the only survivor,
;; so it becomes the If's own incoming control — which is what actually removes the branch.
(defn cproj-idealize [(d (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (match (n-in d 0)
    (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (Some [m]
          (let [t (n-ty m)]
            (if (not (ty-tuple? t))
                (multi-pcopy m idx)
                (if (= (ty-tuple-at t idx) TY-XCTRL)
                    (Some (xctrl-make))
                    (if (and (or (= (n-op m) OP-IF) (= (n-op m) OP-NEVER))
                             (and (= (ty-tuple-len t) 2)
                                  (= (ty-tuple-at t (- 1 idx)) TY-XCTRL)))
                        (n-in m 0)
                        ;; Flip `CProj(If(Not(x)))`: rebuild the If over x with both projections
                        ;; constructed together, then select the opposite arm. The dependency is
                        ;; Simple's `addDep(iff.pred())`; if the predicate later becomes a Not, this
                        ;; projection must be retried.
                        (if (= (n-op m) OP-IF)
                            (match (n-in m 1)
                              (Some [pred]
                                    (do
                                      (n-add-dep! pred d)
                                      (if (= (n-op pred) OP-NOT)
                                          (let [arms
                                                  (if-arms! (if-make (n-in! m 0)
                                                                     (n-in! pred
                                                                            1)))]
                                            (Some (if (= idx 0)
                                                      (arms-false arms)
                                                      (arms-true arms))))
                                          (multi-pcopy m idx))))
                              (None [] (multi-pcopy m idx)))
                            (multi-pcopy m idx)))))))))

(impl NodeOps CProjNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (.name self))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr CProjNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (proj--compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) (.idx self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (cproj-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) (.idx self)))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr CProjNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-CPROJ)
                false
                (= (.idx self)
                   (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr CProjNode) (hdr other))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> i64) (+ 137 (.idx self))))

(impl CFGOps CProjNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; A CProj IS a block head.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

(impl NodeOps ProjNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr ProjNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (.name self))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; Final Simple preserves a HIGH result projection from CallEnd while optimistic call-graph
  ;; linking is still discovering Returns. Replacing it with a detached TOP Constant loses the
  ;; edge that must sharpen later. Other Projs retain ordinary constant folding.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr ProjNode))]
                  (-> bool)
                  (let [d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)]
                    (match (n-in d 0)
                      (Some [m]
                            (and (= (n-op m) OP-CALLEND) (ty-high? (n-ty d))))
                      (None [] false))))
  ;; PINNED: a Proj executes in its multi's block and code motion may not move it.
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr ProjNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr ProjNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (proj--compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) (.idx self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr ProjNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (match (n-in (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0)
              (Some [m] (multi-pcopy m (.idx self)))
              (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr ProjNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-PROJ)
                false
                (= (.idx self)
                   (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ProjNode) (hdr other))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> i64) (+ 139 (.idx self))))

(defn cproj-make [(multi (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64) (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (compiler-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [CProjNode] (alloc/create [CProjNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.idx p) idx)
    (set! (.name p) name)
    (n-init! d OP-CPROJ)
    (n-add-def! d (Some multi))
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

(defn proj-make [(multi (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64) (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (compiler-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [ProjNode] (alloc/create [ProjNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.idx p) idx)
    (set! (.name p) name)
    (n-init! d OP-PROJ)
    (n-add-def! d (Some multi))
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE MULTI WINDOW — the only sanctioned way to build an If's arms
;;
;; An If is IN PROGRESS until ALL of its projections exist. Peephole them one at a time and: the
;; first folds to ~ctrl, the If loses its last use, the kill cascade takes it, and the sibling then
;; rewrites against a corpse whose inputs were cleared but whose type was not — a stale buffer word.
;; Flat, that word happened to be pinned Start and the answer was accidentally right; one level of
;; nesting down it was a dead node.
;;
;; So there is deliberately NO way to make one arm alone. `if-arms!` builds both, then peepholes
;; both, and returns them as a pair.
(defstruct IfArms [(true-arm (dyn NodeOps)) (false-arm (dyn NodeOps))])

(defn if-arms! [(iff (dyn NodeOps))] (-> IfArms)
  (let [t (cproj-make iff IF-TRUE-IDX "True")
        f (cproj-make iff IF-FALSE-IDX "False")
        (mut r) (primitive/zeroed IfArms)]
    ;; BOTH exist before EITHER is peepholed. Keep each across the other's peephole, so a fold on
    ;; one cannot take the other down with it.
    (n-keep! t)
    (n-keep! f)
    (let [t2 (n-peephole! t)
          f2 (n-peephole! f)]
      (n-unkeep! t)
      (n-unkeep! f)
      (set! (.true-arm (mut r)) t2)
      (set! (.false-arm (mut r)) f2)
      (load r))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; RETURN
;;
;; Inputs are control, memory, value and RPC. The enclosing Fun is separate metadata, exactly as in
;; Simple: RPC is a graph edge cleared by inlining; ownership is not.
(const RETURN-CTRL 0)

(const RETURN-MEM 1)

(const RETURN-VALUE 2)

(const RETURN-RPC 3)

(defstruct ReturnNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (fun-nid i64)])

(impl NodeOps ReturnNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Return")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr ReturnNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (let [d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)]
             (ty-tuple-of3 (match (n-in d RETURN-CTRL)
                             (Some [c] (n-ty c))
                             (None [] TY-XCTRL))
                           (match (n-in d RETURN-MEM)
                             (Some [m] (n-ty m))
                             (None [] TY-TOP))
                           (match (n-in d RETURN-VALUE)
                             (Some [v] (n-ty v))
                             (None [] TY-TOP)))))
  (idealize [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  ;; Final Simple permits Return equality only while BOTH owning functions are live. During
  ;; inlining a callee Fun dies before its Return's tuple has finished forwarding; admitting that
  ;; stale Return to GVN can transfer callee ownership onto the caller's otherwise equal Return.
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr ReturnNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-RETURN)
                false
                (let [other-return (primitive/cast (ptr ReturnNode) (hdr other))]
                  (and (= (.fun-nid self) (.fun-nid other-return))
                       (and (match (arena-node (.fun-nid self))
                              (Some [fun] (not (n-dead? fun)))
                              (None [] false))
                            (match (arena-node (.fun-nid other-return))
                              (Some [fun] (not (n-dead? fun)))
                              (None [] false)))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> i64) (+ 149 (.fun-nid self))))

(impl CFGOps ReturnNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; A Return is a block TAIL.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

(defn return-make [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps))
                   (mem (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
                   (val (dyn NodeOps))
                   (rpc (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
                   (fun (Option (dyn NodeOps)))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (compiler-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [ReturnNode] (alloc/create [ReturnNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.fun-nid p) (match fun (Some [f] (n-nid f)) (None [] 0)))
    (n-init! d OP-RETURN)
    (n-add-def! d (Some ctrl))
    (n-add-def! d mem)
    (n-add-def! d (Some val))
    (n-add-def! d rpc)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

(defn return-fun-nid [(ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.fun-nid (primitive/cast (ptr ReturnNode) (hdr ret))))

;; Accessors for the pair `if-arms!` returns.
(defn arms-true [(a IfArms)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (.true-arm a))

(defn arms-false [(a IfArms)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (.false-arm a))
